    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Saka were the eastern branch of the Iranian-speaking pastoral peoples whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>. They never formed a single state but a constellation of tribes spread across the steppes and mountains from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> and the edges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> noted that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> called all Scythians <em>Sakai</em>; the name appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> inscriptions as <em>Saka</em> and in Chinese records as <em>Sai</em>. Archaeologically, the Saka world is usually recognised from the eighth and seventh centuries BCE in cultures such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tasmola-culture">Tasmola culture</a> of central Kazakhstan and the early Saka of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a> (Semirechye), while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldy-bel-culture">Aldy-Bel culture</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a>, whose royal kurgan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arzhan-kurgans">Arzhan 2</a> held thousands of gold objects, belongs to the same early Scythian horizon. Their ancestors are generally traced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> herders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronovo-culture">Andronovo</a> horizon, and the spread of fully mounted nomadism around the start of the first millennium BCE gave them great mobility. Achaemenid inscriptions of the late sixth century BCE distinguish several groups: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-tigraxauda">Saka Tigraxauda</a>, 'with pointed caps', probably east of the Caspian and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-haumavarga">Saka Haumavarga</a>, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferghana-valley">Fergana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a>, whose name is usually linked to the ritual drink <em>hauma</em>; and the Saka Paradraya, 'beyond the sea', the Scythians north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/almaty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almaty.jpg" alt="Almaty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Almaty_K%C3%B6k_T%C3%B6be.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dauren Nabijan, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saka life was built on mobile herding of horses, sheep, goats and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, moving between summer pastures in the mountains and winter camps in sheltered valleys. In well-watered regions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> delta many groups also farmed, and permanent settlements are known alongside seasonal camps. Society was ranked: chieftains and a warrior aristocracy stood above ordinary herders, and their status is displayed in the great kurgans, earth mounds raised over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> burial chambers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issyk-kurgan">Issyk kurgan</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a>, excavated in 1969–1970, held the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-man">Golden Man</a>', a richly dressed burial, usually thought to be a young warrior, covered with some four thousand gold ornaments and accompanied by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> bowl bearing a short inscription in a still undeciphered script. The frozen tombs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pazyryk-burials">Pazyryk</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a>, of the fourth and third centuries BCE and usually linked to the wider Saka-Scythian world, preserved felt hangings, tattooed bodies, wooden carvings and the oldest known knotted pile carpet. Saka art used the steppe animal style of deer, felines, birds of prey and griffins, worked in gold, bronze, wood and felt. Religion probably centred on sky and fire, and horses were sacrificed at burials. The Saka exchanged horses, livestock and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> with oasis towns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and mirrors reached their graves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon.jpg" alt="Battle of Marathon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scene_of_the_Battle_of_Marathon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Steeple Davis, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Saka fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>, using the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">composite bow</a>, the short <em>akinakes</em> sword and battle-axes, and their nobles wore scale armour. Their mobility made them hard to conquer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> relates that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> was killed in 530 BCE fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a>, a people east of the Caspian whom many scholars place within the Saka world, under their queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomyris">Tomyris</a>; the story is literary, but it probably reflects a real Persian defeat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> campaigned against the pointed-cap Saka around 519 BCE and captured their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skunkha">Skunkha</a>, whose figure in a tall pointed hat was added to the end of the line of captive rebels on the Behistun relief. Saka contingents thereafter served the Persian kings. Herodotus records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and Saka broke the Athenian centre at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon">Battle of Marathon</a> in 490 BCE and that Saka horsemen fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> in 479 BCE, and Saka cavalry served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Battle of Gaugamela</a> in 331 BCE. In 329 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and defeated a Saka force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-jaxartes">Battle of the Jaxartes</a>, but made no attempt to conquer the steppe. In the second century BCE, displaced Saka warriors overran parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, killing King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phraates-ii">Phraates II</a> in about 127 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tomyris"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomyris.jpg" alt="Tomyris" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tomyris">Tomyris</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomyris-Castagno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrea del Castagno, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Saka had no single government. Each tribe or tribal union was led by chiefs and kings drawn from leading lineages, whose authority depended on wealth in herds, success in war and the loyalty of armed retinues. Greek writers mention named Saka rulers only occasionally, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomyris">Tomyris</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massagetae">Massagetae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zarinaea">Zarinaea</a>, a Saka queen in the semi-legendary history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a>, while the Behistun inscription names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skunkha">Skunkha</a>, whom Darius replaced with a chief loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a> some Saka lands were attached to the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapies</a>, and the reliefs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apadana">Apadana</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> show Saka delegations in pointed hats bringing horses, garments and bracelets to the king; but Persian control beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> was probably loose and based on alliance. Power within Saka society is best seen in the scale of elite burials, which required the labour of many people and the slaughter of numerous horses, implying chiefs who could command resources across wide areas. Clusters of large kurgans, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/besshatyr-kurgans">Besshatyr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issyk-kurgan">Issyk</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berel">Berel</a> in the Kazakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a>, suggest regional centres of power. After Alexander's campaigns and the weakening of Seleucid control in the east, the Saka of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> were fully independent until the migrations of the second century BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tumshuq"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tumshuq.jpg" alt="Tumshuq" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tumshuq">Tumshuq</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_of_Tumushuke_within_Xinjiang_(China).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Croquant, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Saka carried the art, weapons and horse culture of the steppe across a vast area, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> and the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and the animal style found in their graves influenced metalwork from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordos-plateau">Ordos</a>. After their displacement in the second century BCE, Saka groups moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drangiana">Drangiana</a>, which took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sakastan</a> (modern Sistan), and through the mountains into Gandhara and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. There <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maues">Maues</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azes-i">Azes</a> ruled in the first century BCE, and Saka dynasties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> lasted for centuries: the Western Kshatrapas governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> until about 400 CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Shaka era</a>, which begins in 78 CE and is traditionally associated with Saka rulers, is the basis of India's national calendar. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, Saka languages were written at Khotan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumshuq">Tumshuq</a>; Khotanese preserved a large Buddhist literature until the tenth century and gave the kingdom of Khotan its distinctive culture. In Iranian tradition the hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a> of the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a></em> is lord of Sistan, and some scholars trace his legends to Saka epic. In modern Kazakhstan the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issyk-kurgan">Golden Man of Issyk</a> has become a national symbol and stands on top of the Independence Monument in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/phraates-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phraates-ii.jpg" alt="Phraates II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/phraates-ii">Phraates II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tetradrachm_of_Phraates_II,_minted_at_Seleucia_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group; [1], CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Saka world of the steppe was broken by migrations set off by the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. After the Xiongnu defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Gansu corridor</a> around 176 BCE, the Yuezhi moved west into the Ili valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semirechye">Zhetysu</a> and drove out the Saka who lived there. According to the <em>Han shu</em>, the Sai king fled south across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamir</a> passes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibin">Jibin</a>, usually identified with Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, while many Saka stayed behind under the new rulers. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a> in turn expelled the Yuezhi, the Wusun state absorbed the remaining Saka of the Ili region. Other Saka groups pushed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, where nomadic invaders, whether Saka or Yuezhi is debated, destroyed the Greek city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ai-khanoum">Ai-Khanoum</a> around 145 BCE, and into eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The Parthian kings fought them for decades: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phraates-ii">Phraates II</a> was killed in battle against them in about 127 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-ii-of-parthia">Mithridates II</a> subdued them and appears to have settled them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sakastan</a> by around 100 BCE. From then on the Saka no longer formed an independent steppe world. They lived on as ruling elites in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian kingdoms</a>, as subjects of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and later the Kushans, and as the oasis peoples of Khotan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumshuq">Tumshuq</a>, who were absorbed into Turkic and Islamic states from the eleventh century.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Saka, the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> of the Central Asian steppe, created one of the great horse-nomad cultures of antiquity, known for gold-rich kurgans and animal-style art; they fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and Alexander and, after their dispersal, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian kingdoms</a> whose legacy survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Shaka era</a>.</em></p>

